  "sport": "soccer",
  "title": "Defending long goal kicks",
  "description": "Five against five with goalkeepers on a long, narrow pitch, about 70 × 35 metres. Every time play restarts from a goalkeeper – after a goal, when the ball has gone over the end line or when the keeper has caught it – the keeper kicks long. Before the kick, both teams line up where they think the ball will land. Otherwise it is a free game.\n\nThe team defending against the kick must win the first contest for the ball and have players around it to pick up the second ball. If the defenders win the second ball and score within ten seconds, the goal counts double.\n\nLet the keepers vary: kicks from hand, goal kicks, through the middle or out to a wing. Variation: play with offside if you do in matches. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Read the kick early: one player goes up for the header, the others stand around where the ball may drop."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Stay on the right side of your player – between them and your own goal."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Whoever wins the header heads or flicks it to a team-mate, not just up in the air."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Win the second ball and play forward straight away."
    }
  ],
